Bisphosphonate-Induced Osteonecrosis of the Jaw (ONJ).

### Patient Demographics
* Identifier: Patient J.W.
* Age: 71 years
* Gender: Female
* Weight: 58 kg
* Medical History: Postmenopausal Osteoporosis (6 years), no prior radiation therapy to the head or neck, no active oral malignancy.

### Clinical Chronology
* Year 1 (Month 1): Initiated on Alendronate 70 mg orally once weekly for osteoporosis management.
* Year 4 (Month 3): Patient underwent a routine dental extraction of the lower right second molar.
* Year 4 (Month 6): Patient presented with persistent localized pain, purulent discharge, swelling, and non-healing exposed bone at the extraction site lasting greater than 8 weeks.
* Clinical and Diagnostic Findings at Admission:
  * Physical Exam: Non-healing exposed necrotic bone measuring 1.5 cm in the right mandible with surrounding soft tissue inflammation.
  * Cone Beam Computed Tomography (CBCT): Area of localized osteolysis, cortical bone destruction, and osteosclerosis of the right posterior mandible without evidence of metastatic disease.
  * Systemic Markers: Normal serum calcium, vitamin D, and parathyroid hormone levels.
* Intervention: Immediate permanent cessation of Alendronate; initiated chlorhexidine antimicrobial oral rinses, systemic amoxicillin-clavulanate therapy, and conservative dental debridement.
* Follow-up (Month 6 post-discontinuation): Gradual mucosal coverage of the exposed area; full resolution of purulent discharge and pain.

